<SeoArticle title="Trust Infrastructure for AI Systems | Coreweaver Labs" description="Trust infrastructure answers where a machine claim came from, whether it changed, who authorized it, and whether another system can verify it." canonical={canonical} eyebrow="AI Mastery · trust infrastructure" heading="Trust is a protocol." lede="As software interprets, decides, and acts, trust moves from an aspiration to an interface: origin, authenticity, integrity, and verifiability." section="AI Mastery">
  <p>Trust infrastructure is the layer that lets a person or system inspect a machine decision. It connects the source, the transformation, the authorization, and the resulting record. On the public web, that means canonical pages, authorship, source citations, dates, structured data, and stable links. In agent systems, it also means mandates, checkpoints, escalation, and immutable logs.</p>
  <h2>Four questions</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Public web signal</th><th>Operational signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where did this come from?</td><td>Source citation, author, canonical URL.</td><td>Input provenance and event record.</td></tr><tr><td>Has it changed?</td><td>Date modified, version history, stable references.</td><td>Digest, checkpoint, or append-only state.</td></tr><tr><td>Who authorized it?</td><td>Named publisher and transparent editorial process.</td><td>Mandate chain and bounded authority.</td></tr><tr><td>Can it be checked?</td><td>Visible method, evidence, and linked sources.</td><td>Audit trail, test, and verification result.</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Coreweaver’s <a href="/framework/arm/">ARM Framework</a> applies these ideas to accountable agents. The <a href="/geo-compass/">GEO-COMPASS method</a> applies them to public information. Both are attempts to make the path from claim to evidence legible.</p>
